{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "extra_component",
      "components": ["PR5", "H4"],
      "pins": ["PR5.SDA", "PR5.SCL", "PR5.VCC", "H4.1", "H4.2", "H4.3", "H4.4"],
      "nets": ["NET0074", "NET0010", "+5V"],
      "observed": "On the 'WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES' sheet the TF-Luna LiDAR (PR5) is drawn wired through header H4 onto the main I2C bus: H4 pin1 -> +5V, pin2 -> NET0074 (I2C_SDA), pin3 -> NET0010 (I2C_SCL), pin4 -> GND. This is the only water-height sensing element drawn; no HC-SR04-style ultrasonic sensor appears anywhere on the sheet.",
      "expected": "The specification states the LiDAR is documented but NOT implemented in the r2 wiring: 'the r2 netlist shows PR1 with on_board: no, in_bom: yes, and all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free - i.e., it is not connected to any net in this design', and that the water-level sensing that is actually wired is an ultrasonic sensor on two GPIOs. Wiring the LiDAR onto NET0074/NET0010 adds an un-intended device to the I2C bus and replaces the intended ultrasonic water-level path.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Water Height Sensor: PR5 LiDAR -> H4 PB_1.25mm, pins 2/3 to NET0074 / NET0010 (sheet 1, WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the r2 netlist shows PR1 with on_board: no, in_bom: yes, and all four pins (VCC, SDA, SCL, GND) typed free - i.e., it is not connected to any net in this design"}
      ],
      "impact": "The reservoir-level interface differs from the intended design: a LiDAR rangefinder is loaded onto the main I2C bus (potential address/loading conflict) and the wired ultrasonic water-level sensor described by the spec/firmware is absent, so level sensing will not behave as intended.",
      "confidence": 0.5
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_component_type",
      "components": ["T1", "D6"],
      "pins": ["T1.1", "T1.2", "T1.3"],
      "nets": ["+24V", "NET0005"],
      "observed": "On the POWER TRAIN sheet the high-side 'Reverse polarity protection' switch T1 is an AON6407, which the spec identifies as an 8-DFN N-MOSFET. Its source/drain sit in the +24V input path and its gate (pin1) is clamped to ~12 V by Zener D6 to GND, so with the input at 24 V the N-channel device sees Vgs = 12-24 = -12 V and cannot enhance.",
      "expected": "A high-side reverse-polarity switch requires a P-channel MOSFET (as r1 used, SI2301) or an N-channel with a boosted gate; the specification lists AON6407 only as a low-side ATO-valve switch and describes the 24 V input protection as F1 (fuse) + D2 (TVS), with no high-side N-channel MOSFET.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "POWER TRAIN: 'Reverse polarity protection' T1 AON6407, gate pin1 to NET0005 with D6 12V to GND (sheet 1)"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "T3 - AON6407 (8-DFN N-MOSFET) ... ATO solenoid-valve low-side switch"}
      ],
      "impact": "The intended low-loss reverse-polarity switch never turns on; the 24 V rail is passed only through the body diode (voltage drop, heating, no active protection), so the input behaves differently from the intended design.",
      "confidence": 0.35
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing deviates from the intended r2 design in at least two places: the TF-Luna LiDAR, which the spec documents as unconnected/free, is wired onto the main I2C bus (NET0074/NET0010) as the water-height sensor, and the high-side reverse-polarity switch T1 uses an N-channel AON6407 in a topology where it cannot enhance. The LiDAR wiring is the most clearly evidenced anomaly."
}

